<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>System-level comparison on class-conditional ImageNet <math id="S5.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>. “con.” / “disc.” denote continuous / discrete latent types. <math id="S5.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S5.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> indicate higher / lower is better. “P.T.” represents our proposed tokenizer post-training strategy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>More Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:60%;"><img src="2509.12474v1/tsne-intuition-base.png" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/131;" width="329" height="131" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>T-SNE visualization of latent space of tokenizer trained with and without latent perturbation. Colors and thresholds represent the frequency of tokens being used during inference without perturbation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robust latent space.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S5.F7" title="In 5.3 More Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Image Tokenizer Needs Post-Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we compare the latent space (i.e., codebook) with and without latent perturbation. We colorize the latent tokens with their frequency of use during inference. When truncating tokens at different usage count thresholds, we observe the space constructed with latent perturbation contains many reusable tokens, which acted as key tokens that can be easily modeled, while the remaining tokens serve as supportive tokens providing finer detailed information. In contrast, the latent space without latent perturbation distributes usage more uniformly across tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12474v1/ft-diff.png" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/195;" width="548" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Visualization of <math id="S5.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> image generation before (top) and after (bottom) post-training. Three improvements are observed: (a) OOD mitigation, (b) color fidelity, and (c) detail refinement.</figcaption>
</figure>
